---
import Math from './Math.astro';
import Cite from './Cite.astro';
---
<div class="exhibit phase-lab" data-phase-lab><div class="exhibit-heading"><span class="eyebrow">Experiment 04 / A complexity phase transition</span><span class="exhibit-tag">Marwaha & Sud · 2026</span></div><div class="phase-intro"><h3>The singlet in the local spectrum</h3><div><Math display tex={String.raw`h(s)=-s|\Psi^-\rangle\langle\Psi^-|-|\Psi^+\rangle\langle\Psi^+|`}/><p class="fine-print">Positive-weight sums of this two-qubit interaction on arbitrary graphs; the inverse-polynomial precision ground-energy decision problem.</p></div></div><div class="phase-grid"><div class="energy-stage"><div class="eyebrow">Local energy levels</div><svg viewBox="0 0 470 300" role="img" aria-label="Local energy levels: the singlet has energy minus s, one triplet minus one, and two triplets zero"><line x1="50" x2="50" y1="24" y2="260" stroke="currentColor" opacity=".3"/><text x="25" y="55" class="energy-axis">0</text><text x="12" y="145" class="energy-axis">−1</text><text x="12" y="235" class="energy-axis">−2</text><line x1="70" x2="440" y1="50" y2="50" class="energy-guide"/><line x1="70" x2="440" y1="140" y2="140" class="energy-guide"/><line x1="70" x2="440" y1="230" y2="230" class="energy-guide"/><g data-singlet-level><line x1="80" x2="205" y1="185" y2="185" stroke="#b74d2a" stroke-width="4"/><text x="142" y="210" text-anchor="middle">Ψ⁻ · singlet</text></g><line x1="265" x2="395" y1="140" y2="140" stroke="#315aa8" stroke-width="3"/><text x="330" y="165" text-anchor="middle">Ψ⁺ · triplet</text><line x1="265" x2="320" y1="50" y2="50" stroke="#7c8970" stroke-width="3"/><line x1="340" x2="395" y1="50" y2="50" stroke="#7c8970" stroke-width="3"/><text x="330" y="36" text-anchor="middle">Φ⁺, Φ⁻ · triplets</text></svg></div><div class="phase-readout"><span class="eyebrow" data-phase-status>Proved classification</span><div class="phase-class" data-phase-class>QMA-complete</div><p data-phase-description>Past the crossing, the singlet is the unique local ground state.</p><label class="control-label" for="phase-s">Singlet coefficient s <output data-s-output>1.50</output></label><input id="phase-s" type="range" min="-0.5" max="2" step="0.01" value="1.5"/><div class="range-labels"><span>−0.5</span><span>2</span></div><div class="preset-row"><button data-s="-0.3">s &lt; 0</button><button data-s="0">s = 0</button><button data-s="0.5">0 &lt; s &lt; 1</button><button data-s="1">s = 1</button><button data-s="1.5">s &gt; 1</button></div></div></div><div class="phase-spectrum" aria-label="Complexity regimes"><span>∈ BQP<small>via EPR</small></span><b>EPR<small>∈ BQP</small></b><span>StoqMA</span><b>NP</b><span>QMA</span></div><div class="phase-note" role="status" data-phase-note>Local energies are exactly computed; complexity labels are theorem annotations, not inferred by the simulation.</div><p class="phase-context"><strong>EPR and EPR* are in BQP.</strong> Rayudu and Takahashi’s Lee–Yang gap theorem gives the quantum upper bound, following the framework of Wong, Bravyi, Gosset, and Liu. Whether EPR* is in BPP remains open. <Cite keys={['rayudu-takahashi-lee-yang','wong-bravyi-gosset-liu-lee-yang']}/></p><details class="exhibit-details"><summary>Lee–Yang bound, classification, and singlet conjecture</summary><div class="details-body"><p>This one-parameter slice has energies −s, −1, 0, 0. The labels refer to worst-case many-body promise problems, not the difficulty of diagonalizing this four-dimensional matrix. At s = 0 the interaction is locally unitarily equivalent to the EPR interaction. <Cite keys="marwaha-sud-phase-transition"/></p><h4>Why the Lee–Yang theorem applies to EPR*</h4><p>For the EPR* family in <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}questions/q14/`}>Q14</a>, direct expansion gives</p><Math display tex={String.raw`K_b=\frac b2 I+\frac12(XX+bYY-ZZ),\qquad -1\le b\le1.`}/><p>Removing the known scalar, each positive-weight edge satisfies the Lee–Yang condition <Math tex={String.raw`w^z\ge\max(|w^x|,|w^y|)`}/> for <Math tex={String.raw`-w^zZZ+w^xXX+w^yYY`}/>. This identification is our application of their theorem.</p><p>Rayudu–Takahashi use a uniform field <Math tex={String.raw`-h\sum_i Z_i`}/> with gap at least <Math tex={String.raw`h/4`}/>. Adiabatic preparation and energy estimation, with inverse-polynomial <Math tex="h"/> and energy perturbation at most <Math tex="nh"/>, give BQP containment. This does not require a zero-field gap or prepare a prescribed exact zero-field ground state.</p><p>Marwaha and Sud’s <strong>singlet conjecture</strong> says that, for a symmetric two-local interaction K and any triplet state τ, replacing K by K − |τ⟩⟨τ| gives a positive-weight Hamiltonian problem that is at most as hard. This is a conjecture about making a triplet more favorable. It is stronger than the plotted classification, and its consequences include StoqMA = NP.</p><p class="fine-print">The plotted family is field-free and Bell-diagonal. Exchange symmetry alone does not make every interaction Bell-diagonal. The named conjecture is attributed in its source’s broader wording.</p></div></details></div>
<script>import {initPhaseLabs} from '../scripts/exhibits.mjs';initPhaseLabs();</script>
